        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    A sample is practice, not advice. Label it clearly as an exercise.
  </p>

  <h2 id="methods">Three common approaches, explained simply</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Approach</th>
          <th>Idea</th>
          <th>When it fits</th>
          <th>Main weakness</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Approach"><strong>Cash-flow based</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Idea">Value is the present worth of future cash</td>
          <td data-label="When it fits">Stable, forecastable businesses</td>
          <td data-label="Main weakness">Sensitive to assumptions</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Approach"><strong>Market comparison</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Idea">Value is inferred from similar firms</td>
          <td data-label="When it fits">Many comparable firms exist</td>
          <td data-label="Main weakness">True peers are hard to find</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Approach"><strong>Asset based</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Idea">Value is assets minus liabilities</td>
          <td data-label="When it fits">Asset-heavy or winding-down firms</td>
          <td data-label="Main weakness">Ignores earning power</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Most real work uses more than one approach and explains why they differ.
  </p>

  <h2 id="report">What a good valuation note contains</h2>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table paths-table--wide">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Section</th>
          <th>Content</th>
          <th>Test</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Section"><strong>Purpose</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Content">Why the value is needed and for whom</td>
          <td data-label="Test">Is the use clear?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Section"><strong>Information used</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Content">Accounts, dates and sources</td>
          <td data-label="Test">Could another person trace it?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Section"><strong>Method and reasons</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Content">Why this approach</td>
          <td data-label="Test">Would a sceptic accept the choice?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Section"><strong>Assumptions and range</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Content">Key inputs and what changes if they move</td>
          <td data-label="Test">Is the range honest?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Section"><strong>Limits</strong></td>
          <td data-label="Content">What the note does not cover</td>
          <td data-label="Test">Is anything hidden?</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="money">Money: course fees and the 10 percent test</h2>

  <p>
    As a rule of thumb and not a research finding, keep a degree or course fee near a tenth of the family's total education budget, and treat spending above roughly a fifth as a serious caution that needs course-specific evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    An illustration, not a real fee: on a Rs 8 lakh education budget, a tenth is Rs 80,000 for the main course, and a fifth is Rs 1.6 lakh as a serious caution. The rest can fund a laptop, practice time and a reserve.
  </p>
  <p>
    Test the field with free courses and one finished project before paying for a long programme. Do not borrow heavily for a course whose graduates cannot show placement in a specific role.
  </p>

